== ch04-020 ch4 sentences
  [0] Just consider the number of technologies in Matt Turck’s (in)famous depictions of the ML, AI, and data (MAD) landscape that we introduced in Chapter 1 (Figure 4-1).
  [1] Even relatively successful technologies often fade into obscurity quickly, after a few years of rapid adoption, a victim of their success.
  [2] For instance, in the early 2010s, Hive was met with rapid uptake because it allowed both analysts and engineers to query massive datasets without coding complex MapReduce jobs by hand.
  [3] Inspired by the success of Hive but wishing to improve on its shortcomings, engineers developed Presto and other technologies.
  [4] Hive now appears primarily in legacy deployments.
  [5] Almost every technology follows this inevitable path of decline.

== ch04-019 ch4 sentences
  [0] What’s the popular frontend JavaScript framework three years from now?
  [1] New well-funded entrants and open source projects arrive on the data front every day.
  [2] Every vendor will say their product will change the industry and “make the world a better place”.
  [3] Most of these companies and projects don’t get long-term traction and fade slowly into obscurity.
  [4] Top VCs are making big-money bets, knowing that most of their data-tooling investments will fail.
  [5] If VCs pouring millions (or billions) into data-tooling investments can’t get it right, how can you possibly know which technologies to invest in for your data architecture?
  [6] Just consider the number of technologies in Matt Turck’s (in)famous depictions of the ML, AI, and data (MAD) landscape that we introduced in Chapter 1 (Figure 4-1).

